  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Wed Jun 24 2026\n\nNo major changes were made to this afternoon forecast package. One \nthing to note will be that a batch of Saharan dust will be moving \ninto the Gulf this weekend with the NASA GEOS forecast calling for \nthis dust to move into South Texas by the start of the upcoming work \nweek. This will bring hazy/milky skies, but it is too early to tell \nif the concentration would be high enough to reduce temperatures \nfrom NBM guidance right now. We will keep monitoring this trend.\n\n...Previous Discussion...\n\nHigh pressure will dominate the forecast through the upcoming week, \nleading to dry and hot weather. Heat indices will generally range \nfrom 100-109 across South Texas with a Moderate HeatRisk for most of \nSouth Texas. Remember to apply and reapply the sunscreen/mosquito \nspray/lotions, remain hydrated, and to take plenty of breaks in the \nshade/AC. \n\nAside from the heat, this weekend's winds will become breezier \n(southeast winds at 20-25 mph gusting to 35 mph). Most of the area \nwill likely welcome the breeze, but we will be considering marine \nhazards by this weekend.",
